  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over Saskatchewan, with \nhigh over the Pacific Northwest. Upper level analysis places low\nnear the California/Nevada/Arizona border. Westerly flow \nremains over our area with a potent short wave pushing off to \nthe east. Over our area, quiet weather is now in place as most \nof the low clouds from earlier have diminished. \n\nFor tonight, a quiet night is on tap as surface low pushes off\nover Manitoba. This will keep a light westerly wind going\nthrough the night.\n\nOn Wednesday, westerly flow remains in place with dry conditions\ncontinuing. A cooler surface high starts to push in later in the\nday into the night. Therefore, lows in the mid to upper 30s are\npossible mainly over parts of northwestern North Dakota which\ncould lead to some frost.\n\nOn Thursday, a weak wave skirts southwestern North Dakota which\ncould bring a shower to the area. Otherwise, quiet and cooler\nweather continues. Surface high passes over overnight with winds\ngoing very light. This will allow temperatures to drop even\nmore, with lows possibly dipping into the mid 30s over much of\nwestern and central North Dakota. Therefore, this could be our\nfirst widespread frost of the year. \n\nTo end the week into the weekend, upper flow shifts more\nwest/northwest bringing seasonal temperatures. A few weak waves\nmove through the flow possibly bringing a few light showers, but\nchances are low (about 20 to 30 percent).",
